<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Yang of Sui</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">569–<a href="/years/618">618 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Emperor Yang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Yang was the second emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui dynasty</a>. He undertook vast projects, including the completion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a>, and launched costly failed campaigns against the Korean kingdom of Goguryeo. The resulting exhaustion and revolts brought down the Sui and opened the way for the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui.jpg" alt="Emperor Yang of Sui" width="268" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yan Liben · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sui_Yangdi_Tang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Goguryeo's defences met their greatest test in the wars with Sui and Tang: Sui Wendi's expedition of 598 failed, <b>Sui Yangdi</b>'s huge army of 612 was routed by Eulji Mundeok at the Salsu River, and in 645 Tang Taizong took Liaodong fortress but abandoned the siege of Ansi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Emperor Wen and his successor <b>Emperor Yang</b> pursued centralising reforms, most notably the equal-field system, which aimed to reduce economic inequality and raise agricultural productivity, the Five Departments and Six Boards system that preceded the Tang's Three Departments and Six Ministries, and the standardisation and reunification of the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/sui-dynasty">Sui Dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…Tang and a Sui military commander at Taiyuan, who exploited the collapse of the Sui, brought down by <b>Emperor Yang</b>'s disastrous campaigns against the Korean kingdom of Goguryeo (the Goguryeo–Sui War) and the massive rebellions they provoked, to seize the capital Chang'an in 617 and proclaim a new…</blockquote><a href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Chi Tu is known almost entirely from the Sui shu, which describes a mission sent by <b>Emperor Yang of Sui</b> in 607 under Chang Jun.</blockquote><a href="/state/chi-tu">Chi Tu</a></li><li><blockquote>During the collapse of the Sui, Shibi Khagan besieged <b>Emperor Yang</b> at Yanmen in 615, and his successors backed Chinese rebels and threatened the new Tang dynasty, reaching the Wei River near Chang'an in 626.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-turkic-khaganate">Eastern Turkic Khaganate</a></li><li><blockquote>Xiao Kui, the second emperor, was a respected scholar, and his daughter married <b>Yang Guang</b>, the future Emperor Yang of the Sui Dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/western-liang-555-587">Western Liang (555-587)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.31,108.86&amp;year=600" aria-label="Chang'an on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7319 921 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Emperor Yang of Sui"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7769 1191h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7769 1191h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.31,108.86&amp;year=600">What was here in 600 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The evening river is level and motionless— The spring colours just open to their full. Suddenly a wave carries the moon…&#8221;</p><footer>Poem on the river at evening &#183; tr. Arthur Waley, 1918 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/42290/pg42290-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
